we've all seen it - gas stations on the  same street selling the same product but  at different prices variety and gas  prices can dictate the commuters route  to work or impact the cost of a family's  summer vacation Americans are keenly  aware of the impact gas prices can have  on their lives but all drivers have  probably wondered at some point  why do gas prices vary so much from  state to state town to town and  sometimes even block-to-block overall  gas prices are dictated by a combination  of global national and local economic  forces they vary depending on how those  forces add up we'll explain here's a  breakdown of where your money goes at  the pump the biggest factor that impacts  gas prices is the one you probably  expect the price of crude oil this  doesn't explain price differences from  pump to pump but it does explain why the  price of a gallon of gas can fluctuate  from day to day changes and gas prices  follow changes in oil prices oil prices  fluctuate for a variety of reasons first  they depend on the output from  oil-producing countries like the top  ones shown here if a major oil producer  like Saudi Arabia cuts production the  price could rise in fact the  Organization of Petroleum Exporting  Countries or OPEC sometimes does this on  purpose to raise the price of crude oil  other factors that affect the cost of  crude oil include speculation and  financial markets the amount of  stockpiled oil reserves geopolitical and  weather events and demand the United  States has the highest demand for crude  oil in the world consuming nineteen  point six million barrels per day this  demand is seasonal US oil demand  increases over the first half of the  year and peaks in summer months as  travel increases summer travel often  involves crossing state lines and gas  prices can vary substantially from state  to state those differences mostly have  to do with state taxes and refining  methods  there's a federal excise tax that  everyone pays but each state also levies  their own gas tax Pennsylvania drivers  pay over seventy seven cents per gallon  in taxes the nation's highest while  drivers in Alaska pay the lowest in  taxes at just over 32 cents per gallon  some of these taxes go to state  departments of transportation and over  the past decade more than two dozen  states have increased their tax rates to  fund infrastructure projects beyond  taxes fuel additives and refining  processes create price differences  between states most of the fuel sold and  the u.s. is ten percent ethanol coastal  states may see a 2 to 3 cent gas price  increase to cover the transportation  costs to ship the additive from the  nation's corn belt some states are also  required by law to use special  reformulated gasoline that reduces air  pollution it's currently used in 18  states and makes up about 30% of the  fuel sold in the US were formulated  gasoline was almost 30 cents more on  average than conventional gas in 2018  California has one of the highest gas  taxes and most stringent were formulated  gas programs in the country leading the  prices consistently above the national  average also very few facilities outside  of the state are equipped to refine its  specialized fuel when two of the state's  13 were formulated gas refineries  experienced outages in 2015  fuel had to be imported from overseas  driving up the price but what about that  few cents difference between neighboring  gas stations that often comes down to  marketing decisions made by station  owners the majority of gas stations in  the United States are privately owned  businesses station owners negotiate  contracts with fuel suppliers that cover  shipment frequency volume in branding  disruptions up the supply chain like a  refinery shutdown can impact stations  differently depending on the terms of  their contract which can lead to  different prices at different locations  but also profit margins on gas are low  compared to other goods sold at gas  stations like food and drinks after  expenses a station will take in an  average of 10 to 15 cents for  every gallon of gas sold according to an  industry trade group fuel accounts for a  majority of sales at gas stations but  only about 1/3 of profits station owners  make most of their money from in-store  purchases in a recent industry survey  over half of drivers said that the gas  price is the biggest factor when  deciding where to fill up a slightly  lower gas prices can increase customer  traffic to a store creating more  opportunities for owners to sell those  higher margin in-store items the cost of  a gallon of gas at any time is a  reflection of multiple economic forces  both large and small but gas stations  will always try to find a way to get  drivers to go that extra mile
 

